", "name": "Zulu"}, "word": "ugwadule class 11/10"}], "subsenses": [{"definition": "In particular, a barren, arid area of land which is hot, with sandy, rocky, or parched ground.", "tags": ["countable", "uncountable"], "examples": [], "quotes": [{"text": "It is wholly out of the power of language to convey any idea of the blissful enjoyment of obtaining water, after an almost total want of it, during eight and forty hours, in the scorching regions of an Arabian desert, in the month of July.", "reference": "1805 December, Julius Griffiths, “A Journey across the Desert”, in The Monthly Mirror, page 362:"}, {"text": "The desert storm was riding in its strength; the travellers lay beneath the mastery of the fell simoom. Whirling wreaths and columns of burning wind, rushed around and over them.", "reference": "1892, James Yoxall, chapter 5, in The Lonely Pyramid:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}]}, {"definition": "(figuratively) Any barren place or situation.", "tags": ["countable", "figuratively", "uncountable"], "examples": [], "quotes": [{"text": "He declared that the country was an intellectual desert; that he was famishing for spiritual aliment, and for discourse on matters beyond mere nuggets, prospectings, and the price of gold.", "reference": "1858, William Howitt, Land, Labour, and Gold; Or, Two Years in Victoria, page 54:"}, {"text": "By contrast, the WR route is an economic desert between Newbury and Taunton.", "reference": "1964 March, “News and Comment: Which way to the West?”, in Modern Railways, page 147:"}, {"text": "So the question that is commonly asked is, why put a media incubator in a media desert and have it managed by a civil servant?", "reference": "2006, Philip N. Cooke, Creative Industries in Wales: Potential and Pitfalls, page 34:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [{"language": {"code": "hu", "name": "Hungarian"}, "word": "puszta"}, {"language": {"code": "hu", "name": "Hungarian"}, "word": "pusztaság"}, {"language": {"code": "hu", "name": "Hungarian"}, "word": "posvány"}], "subsenses": []}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": []}, {"language": {"code": "en", "name": "English"}, "partOfSpeech": "adjective", "pronunciations": [{"type": "ipa", "text": "/ˈdɛz.ət/", "tags": ["Received Pronunciation"]}, {"type": "ipa", "text": "/ˈdɛz.ɚt/", "tags": ["General American"]}, {"type": "ipa", "text": "/dəˈzɑɹt/", "tags": []}], "forms": [], "senses": [{"definition": "Usually of a place: abandoned, deserted, or uninhabited.", "tags": ["not comparable"], "examples": ["They were marooned on a desert island in the Pacific."], "quotes": [{"text": "And he said vnto them, Come yee your selues apart into a desert place, and rest a while. For there were many comming and going, and they had no leisure so much as to eate.", "reference": "1611, The Holy Bible, […] (King James Version), London: […] Robert Barker, […], →OCLC, Mark vi:31:"}, {"text": "He […] went aside privately into a desert place.", "reference": "1611, The Holy Bible, […] (King James Version), London: […] Robert Barker, […], →OCLC, Luke ix:10:"}, {"text": "See, from afar, yon Rock that mates the Sky, / About whoſe Feet ſuch Heaps of Rubbiſh lye: / Such indigeſted Ruin; bleak and bare, / How deſart now it ſtands, expos'd in Air!", "reference": "1697, Virgil, “The Eighth Book of the Æneis”, in John Dryden, transl., The Works of Virgil: Containing His Pastorals, Georgics, and Æneis. […], London: […] Jacob Tonson, […], →OCLC, page 441, lines 252–255:"}, {"text": "Full many a flower is born to blush unseen, / And waste its sweetness on the desert air.", "reference": "1750, Thomas Gray, Elegy Written in a Country Churchyard, Stanza 14:"}], "synonyms": ["forsaken", "peopleless", "vacant", "abandoned", "derelict", "desert", "deserted", "desolate", "discarded", "dropped", "forlorn", "forsaken", "high and dry", "left behind", "orphaned", "beingless", "desert", "deserted", "desolate", "disinhabited", "inhabitantless", "inhabi