ya"}, {"language": {"code": "tl", "name": "Tagalog"}, "word": "tumanan"}, {"language": {"code": "tl", "name": "Tagalog"}, "word": "tumakas"}, {"language": {"code": "th", "name": "Thai"}, "word": "ทิ้ง"}, {"language": {"code": "th", "name": "Thai"}, "word": "ตัดหางปล่อยวัด"}, {"language": {"code": "ota", "name": "Ottoman Turkish"}, "word": "براقمق"}, {"language": {"code": "yi", "name": "Yiddish"}, "word": "אַוועקוואַרפֿן"}], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "To leave one's duty or post, especially to leave a military or naval unit without permission.", "tags": [], "examples": ["Anyone found deserting will be punished."], "quotes": [], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": []}, {"language": {"code": "en", "name": "English"}, "partOfSpeech": "noun", "pronunciations": [{"type": "ipa", "text": "/dɪˈzɜːt/", "tags": ["Received Pronunciation"]}, {"type": "ipa", "text": "/dɪˈzɝt/", "tags": ["General American"]}, {"type": "ipa", "text": "/dəˈzɝt/", "tags": ["General American"]}], "forms": [{"word": "deserts", "tags": ["plural"]}], "senses": [{"definition": "(often in the plural; now chiefly technical (in philosophy) or fossil) That which is deserved or merited; a just punishment or reward.", "tags": ["in plural", "often"], "examples": ["just deserts", "July 4, 1789, Alexander Hamilton, Eulogium on Major-General Greene\nHis reputation falls far below his desert."], "quotes": [{"text": "From the highest spire of contentment / my fortune is thrown; / and fear and grief and pain for my deserts / are my hopes, since hope is gone.", "reference": "1600, John Dowland, Flow My Tears:"}, {"text": "Who will believe my verse in time to come,\nIf it were fill'd with your most high deserts?", "reference": "1609, William Shakespeare, “Sonnet 17”, in Shake-speares Sonnets. […], London: By G[eorge] Eld for T[homas] T[horpe] and are to be sold by William Aspley, →OCLC:"}, {"text": "\"Nonsense, Mina. It is a shame to me to hear such a word. I would not hear it of you. And I shall not hear it from you. May God judge me by my deserts, and punish me with more bitter suffering than even this hour, if by any act or will of mine anything ever come between us!\"", "reference": "1897, Bram Stoker, Dracula, Westminster [London]: Archibald Constable and Company, […], →OCLC:"}, {"text": "\"It is true that certain common sense precepts of justice, particularly those which concern the protection of liberties and rights, or which express the claims of desert, seem to contradict this contention.\"", "reference": "1971, John Rawls, A Theory of Justice:"}, {"text": "\"For Aristotle, justice means giving people what they deserve, giving each person his or her due. But what is a person due? What are the relevant grounds of merit or desert?\"", "reference": "2009, Michael J. Sandel, Justice: What's the Right Thing to Do?:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [{"language": {"code": "ar", "name": "Arabic"}, "word": "جَزاء"}, {"language": {"code": "hy", "name": "Armenian"}, "word": "վաստակ"}, {"language": {"code": "bg", "name": "Bulgarian"}, "word": "заслу́жено"}, {"language": {"code": "da", "name": "Danish"}, "word": "fortjeneste"}, {"language": {"code": "fi", "name": "Finnish"}, "word": "ansio"}, {"language": {"code": "fi", "name": "Finnish"}, "word": "palkka"}, {"language": {"code": "ka", "name": "Georgian"}, "word": "დამსახურება"}, {"language": {"code": "de", "name": "German"}, "word": "Verdienst"}, {"language": {"code": "he", "name": "Hebrew"}, "word": "גְּמוּל"}, {"language": {"code": "hu", "name": "Hungarian"}, "word": "érdem"}, {"language": {"code": "ja", "name": "Japanese"}, "word": "応分"}, {"language": {"code": "ko", "name": "Korean"}, "word": "응분"}, {"language": {"code": "lv", "name": "Latvian"}, "word": "nopelns"}, {"language": {"code": "enm", "name": "Middle English"}, "word": "desert"}, {"language": {"code": "enm", "name": "Middle English"}, "word": "hire"}, {"language": {"code": "pt", "name": "Portuguese"}, "word": "merecimento"}, {"language": {"code": "pt", "name": "Portuguese"}, "word": "mérito"}, {"language": {"code": "ru", "name": "Russian"}, "word": "заслу́женное"}, {"language": {"code": "es", "name": "Spanish"}, "word": "merecido"}], "subsenses": []}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": []}, {"language": {"code": "en", "name": "English"}, "partOfSpeech": "noun", "pronunciations": [], "forms": [{"word": "deserts", "tags": ["plural"]}], "senses": [{"definition": "Obsolete form of dessert.", "tags": ["alt of", "countable", "obsolete", "uncountable"], "examples": [], "quotes": [{"text": "Francis, besides being an excellent Cook, knowing how to provide genteel Dinners, and giving aid in dressing them, prepared the Desert, made the Cake, and did every thing that Hyde & wife conjointly do;—[…]", "reference": "1790 September 20, George Washington, Letters from George Washington to Tobias Lear: With an Appendix Containing Miscellaneous Washington Letters and Documents; Reprinted from the Originals in the Collection of Mr. William K. Bixby, of St. Louis, Mo.; With Introduction and Notes, Rochester, N.Y., published 1905, page 8:"}, {"text": "They answer for caramel or gum paste baskets, for desert or suppers. […] BARBERRIES / For Deserts, or Second Course Pastry. […] Under these impressions—admitting, as they would, such a variety of combinations, the Author has confined himself to a plain selection of the principal articles in season in each month, merely to give a good general idea of laying out a table for deserts, leaving to the house[-]keeper to vary, and increase or diminish, according to her taste, or the extent of her company.", "reference": "1816, John Simpson, A Complete System of Cookery, on a Plan Entirely New; Consisting of an Extensive and Original Collection of Receipts, in Cookery, Confectionary, etc. […], London: […] W. Stewart, […]; Baldwin, Cradock, and Joy, […]; Gale and Fenner, […], pages 518, 525, and 541:"}, {"text": "The deserts are far more superb; the painter, the florist, the decorator, and even the sculptor being engaged to complete them. Formerly a desert at a splendid fete in a private house has cost a thousand pounds, exclusive of plate and glass.", "reference": "1818, Peter Hervé, How to Enjoy Paris; Being a Complete Guide to the Visiter of the French Metropolis. […], 2nd edition, London: […] the Author: […] Mr. Egerton, Whitehall; Messrs. Hoitt, […]; and Mesers.^([sic]) Sherwood, Neely, and Jones, […], page 286:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": []}], "source": {"url": "https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/desert", "license": {"name": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/"}}}}